  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Study protected horticulture Bangladesh</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The primary objective of this study is to identify best practices and innovative solutions for sustainable development of (semi-)controlled and protected production methods of vegetables (Greenhouse Farming: low-, mid- and high-tech) in Bangladesh and to trigger Dutch and Bangladeshi partners for the development and introduction of new technologies in the Bangladeshi food value chains. Moreover, identification of adequate and effective technologies for the local covered horticulture situation contributes to local food security and sustainable economic development ambitions in line with vision &#x2018;Bangladesh Delta Plan 2100&#x2019;. Both Bangladeshi and Dutch companies will benefit from creating jobs in the horticultural sector and introducing innovations to the horticultural supply chain, resulting in the sustainable development of the emerging protected horticulture sector in Bangladesh.The outcome will:1.	Equip Dutch companies and institutes, active within horticultural value chains, with information (i.e. current practices relevant for business cases) to promote Bangladesh&#x2019;s protected horticultural sector through: co-investments, innovation and knowledge sharing.2.	Provide further insight and pave way for interventions to support relevant supervisory, facilitating and regulatory bodies and key chain actors in Bangladesh.3.	Identify incentives and opportunities to attract foreign direct investments for Bangladesh.4.	Provide an overview of laws, regulations and policies that are important to know for foreign investors.5.	Ideally, paving the way for new project proposals for vocational training (along the protected horticulture supply chain), investment opportunities (with a.o. ADB/WB/FMO/II/FAO), logistical management, and upgrading existing facilities.</narrative>
  </description>
